latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the Grid:\n   a. Iterate through the entire grid.\n   b. Identify all connected red (2) regions using a flood fill algorithm.\n   c. For each region, calculate its size (number of cells) and complexity (perimeter length).\n   d. Store information about each region, including its quadrant location.\n\n2. Determine the Starting Quadrant:\n   a. Compare all red regions based on a combination of size and complexity.\n   b. Identify the most significant region (largest size and/or most complex).\n   c. Determine which quadrant this region belongs to (top-left, top-right, bottom-left, or bottom-right).\n\n3. Define the Color Transformation Cycle:\n   a. Set up the cycle: Red (2) -> Green (3) -> Sky Blue (8) -> Red (2)\n\n4. Apply the Color Transformation:\n   a. Starting from the quadrant with the most significant region, apply the color cycle counterclockwise.\n   b. Create a mapping of quadrants to new colors based on this cycle.\n\n5. Create the New Grid:\n   a. Initialize a new grid with the same dimensions as the input grid.\n   b. Iterate through the original grid:\n      - If the cell is black (0), keep it black in the new grid.\n      - If the cell is red (2), apply the new color based on its quadrant using the mapping from step 4.\n\n6. Handle Edge Cases:\n   a. Ensure that the bottom-right quadrant is never sky blue (8).\n   b. If the cycle would make the bottom-right sky blue, swap its color with the next quadrant counterclockwise.\n\n7. Implement the Solution:\n   a. Write a main function `solve_fea12743` that takes a ColoredGrid as input and returns a new ColoredGrid.\n   b. Implement helper functions for each major step (e.g., find_regions, determine_start_quadrant, apply_color_cycle).\n\n8. Test and Refine:\n   a. Run the solution against the provided test cases.\n   b. Verify that the output matches the expected results for all examples.\n   c. Refine the algorithm if any discrepancies are found.\n\nHere's a more detailed breakdown of some key steps:\n\nFor step 1 (Analyze the Grid):\n```python\ndef find_regions(grid: ColoredGrid) -> List[Region]:\n    regions = []\n    visited = set()\n    for r in range(grid.num_rows):\n        for c in range(grid.num_cols):\n            if grid.values[r][c] == 2 and (r, c) not in visited:\n                region = flood_fill(grid, r, c)\n                size = len(region)\n                complexity = calculate_complexity(region)\n                quadrant = determine_quadrant(r, c, grid.num_rows, grid.num_cols)\n                regions.append(Region(size, complexity, quadrant, region))\n                visited.update(region)\n    return regions\n```\n\nFor step 2 (Determine the Starting Quadrant):\n```python\ndef determine_start_quadrant(regions: List[Region]) -> str:\n    return max(regions, key=lambda r: r.size * 0.7 + r.complexity * 0.3).quadrant\n```\n\nFor step 4 (Apply the Color Transformation):\n```python\ndef apply_color_cycle(start_quadrant: str) -> Dict[str, int]:\n    quadrants = ['top-left', 'top-right', 'bottom-right', 'bottom-left']\n    colors = [2, 3, 8, 2]\n    start_index = quadrants.index(start_quadrant)\n    return {q: colors[(i + start_index) % 4] for i, q in enumerate(quadrants)}\n```\n\nFor step 5 (Create the New Grid):\n```python\ndef create_new_grid(original_grid: ColoredGrid, color_mapping: Dict[str, int]) -> ColoredGrid:\n    new_grid = ColoredGrid(values=[[0 for _ in range(original_grid.num_cols)] for _ in range(original_grid.num_rows)])\n    for r in range(original_grid.num_rows):\n        for c in range(original_grid.num_cols):\n            if original_grid.values[r][c] == 2:\n                quadrant = determine_quadrant(r, c, original_grid.num_rows, original_grid.num_cols)\n                new_grid.values[r][c] = color_mapping[quadrant]\n            else:\n                new_grid.values[r][c] = original_grid.values[r][c]\n    return new_grid\n```\n\nThis plan should provide a consistent approach to solving the challenge for all given examples and should be flexible enough to handle new cases as well."
